{#
  The page every other layout extends.

  The shell of the andrewshell.org design (decision-16): one wrapper at the
  measure, a big site title and tagline on the front page and a small link home
  everywhere else, and a footer holding the copyright, the colophon and the
  site's own links. `data-is-root-path` on the wrapper is how the stylesheet
  tells the front page from every other one.

  One home for each kind of link (TASK-105). The header carries the site menu,
  on every page. `partials/bio.njk` carries the person whose page or post it
  is. The footer carries `menus.footer`, the site's own list, and nothing read
  off an account. The search form is on the search page and nowhere else.

  Blocks a site can override without copying the whole file:
    title             the contents of <title>
    speculationRules  the <script type="speculationrules"> that prefetches the
                      site's own pages; empty it to prefetch nothing
    viewTransitions   the <style> that opts into cross-document view
                      transitions; empty it to turn them off
    head              the cards, the icons and the structured data, at the end
                      of <head>. Call `super()` from an override that only
                      adds to it.
    alternates        <link rel="alternate"> tags, where the feeds go
    header            the site header, with the site menu
    breadcrumbs       the trail at the top of <main>; empty it to print none
                      (the JSON-LD keeps its BreadcrumbList)
    content           the page body
    footer            the site footer
    scripts           scripts at the end of <body>
#}{% import "partials/feeds.njk" as feeds %}
{%- import "partials/menu.njk" as nav %}
{#- Whether this is the front page, which is the one page the header and the
    stylesheet treat differently. `page.url` is the URL being served rather
    than the document's own permalink, so a static homepage under the Reading
    settings is the root path too. -#}
{% set atRoot = (page.url or url or "/") == "/" %}
{#- What the head says this page is, in one place, because the description
    tags, the Open Graph tags and the Twitter card all say the same thing.

    `metaDescription` is the page's own description, else the entry's summary
    — the very line the feeds publish — else the site's tagline. `metaImage` is
    `shareImage.url`: the picture the front matter names, else the site's
    avatar; with neither, nothing is printed rather than an empty tag. The CMS
    works out `shareImage` because its alt text and its size live in files a
    template cannot read. `isEntry` is a rendered post
    or page, which is what Open Graph calls an article: a listing has
    `pagination`, and the posts page is a listing carrying a page's front
    matter, so it is a website like every other listing. -#}
{% set metaDescription = description or summary or site.tagline %}
{#- A note has no title, and is named here by its first words. -#}
{% set pageTitle = title or label %}
{% set metaTitle = pageTitle or site.title %}
{% set metaUrl = (page.url or "/") | absoluteUrl %}
{% set metaImage = shareImage.url if shareImage else "" %}
{% set isEntry = (type == "post" or type == "page") and not pagination %}
{#- An author archive is the only page that is about a person: a listing whose
    `author` is whose page this is, rather than the posts page's writer. -#}
{% set isAuthorArchive = pagination and author.url and url and (author.url in url) %}
{#- Where this page sits, as `{ name, url }` from the front page down to it
    (TASK-150): a post under its first category, and an archive under the
    front page, with a crumb of its own past page one. The visible breadcrumb
    and the JSON-LD both print this list, so they cannot say different things.
    Every other page has no hierarchy worth showing and an empty trail. -#}
{% set crumbs = [] %}
{% if pagination and category %}
{% set _ = crumbs.push({ "name": category, "url": "/" + (site.categoryBase or "category") + "/" + (category | urlencode) + "/" }) %}
{% elif pagination and tag %}
{% set _ = crumbs.push({ "name": tag, "url": "/" + (site.tagBase or "tag") + "/" + (tag | urlencode) + "/" }) %}
{% elif isAuthorArchive %}
{% set _ = crumbs.push({ "name": author.name, "url": author.url }) %}
{% elif type == "post" and isEntry and categories.length %}
{% set _ = crumbs.push({ "name": categories[0], "url": "/" + (site.categoryBase or "category") + "/" + (categories[0] | urlencode) + "/" }) %}
{% set _ = crumbs.push({ "name": pageTitle, "url": page.url or url }) %}
{% endif %}
{% if crumbs.length and pagination.pageNumber > 0 %}
{% set _ = crumbs.push({ "name": "Page " + (pagination.pageNumber + 1), "url": page.url or url }) %}
{% endif %}
{% set breadcrumbs = [{ "name": "Home", "url": "/" }].concat(crumbs) if crumbs.length else [] %}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ site.language | default("en") }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    {#
      The colours the theme's `theme.json` declares (TASK-146). The colour
      scheme goes before the stylesheet, so a reader in dark mode gets a dark
      canvas while the stylesheet is still on its way rather than a white
      flash. The theme colour tints the browser around the page, one per
      scheme, and is the page's own background so the two meet without a seam.
    #}
    {% if theme.colorScheme %}
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="{{ theme.colorScheme }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% for scheme in ["light", "dark"] %}
    {% if theme.themeColor[scheme] %}
    <meta name="theme-color" content="{{ theme.themeColor[scheme] }}" media="(prefers-color-scheme: {{ scheme }})">
    {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
    <title>{% block title %}{% if pageTitle %}{{ pageTitle }} &middot; {% endif %}{{ site.title }}{% endblock %}</title>
    {% if metaDescription %}
    <meta name="description" content="{{ metaDescription }}">
    {% endif %}
    <link rel="canonical" href="{{ (page.url or "/") | absoluteUrl }}">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ "style.css" | asset }}">
    {% block speculationRules %}
    {#
      Prefetch the page behind a link the reader is about to follow: a hover
      or a press on one of this site's own pages (TASK-140). Not the admin,
      logout, a feed, an upload or a URL with a query, which are not pages to
      read or not safe to fetch unasked. Every pattern goes through `url`, so
      a site in a subdirectory prefetches only its own pages. The CMS drops
      this script from a page drawn for somebody signed in.
    #}
    {% set speculationRules = { "prefetch": [{
      "source": "document",
      "eagerness": "moderate",
      "where": { "and": [
        { "href_matches": "/*" | url },
        { "not": { "href_matches": "/admin" | url } },
        { "not": { "href_matches": "/admin/*" | url } },
        { "not": { "href_matches": "/*logout*" | url } },
        { "not": { "href_matches": "/feed/*" | url } },
        { "not": { "href_matches": "/*/feed/*" | url } },
        { "not": { "href_matches": "/uploads/*" | url } },
        { "not": { "href_matches": "/*\\?(.+)" | url } }
      ] }
    }] } %}
    <script type="speculationrules">{{ speculationRules | dump | safe }}</script>
    {% endblock %}
    {% block viewTransitions %}
    {#
      Cross-document view transitions: the default cross-fade between two of
      this site's pages, and none for a reader who asks for reduced motion.
    #}
    <style>
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
        @view-transition { navigation: auto; }
      }
    </style>
    {% endblock %}
    {% block alternates %}
    {{ feeds.feedLinks("/", site.title) }}
    {{ feeds.commentsFeedLink("/comments/feed/", site.title + " comments") }}
    {% if commentsFeed %}
    {{ feeds.commentsFeedLink(commentsFeed, "Comments on: " + pageTitle) }}
    {% endif %}
    {% if activityStreams %}
    <link rel="alternate" type="application/activity+json" href="{{ activityStreams }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% endblock %}
    {% if webmention %}
    <link rel="webmention" href="{{ webmention | url }}">
    {% endif %}
    {#- The site's index for language models (TASK-149), from the front page,
        which is where a tool that found the site looks for it. #}
    {% if llmsTxt and atRoot %}
    <link rel="describedby" type="text/markdown" href="{{ llmsTxt | url }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% block head %}
    {#
      What a link to this page looks like when somebody posts it: the same
      title, description and picture to Open Graph and to a Twitter card,
      which is the pair every reader of this markup understands. The image
      always carries its alt text, and its size when the CMS knows it. The
      card is `summary_large_image` only for a picture wide enough to fill
      one, and `summary` — a small picture beside the words — for the site's
      avatar and anything else.

      An entry is an article, with its times, its author and its tags: the
      author is their profile here when they have one, else their name.
    #}
    <meta property="og:title" content="{{ metaTitle }}">
    {% if metaDescription %}
    <meta property="og:description" content="{{ metaDescription }}">
    {% endif %}
    <meta property="og:url" content="{{ metaUrl }}">
    <meta property="og:type" content="{% if isEntry %}article{% else %}website{% endif %}">
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ site.title }}">
    {% if shareImage %}
    <meta property="og:image" content="{{ shareImage.url | absoluteUrl }}">
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ shareImage.alt }}">
    {% if shareImage.size %}
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="{{ shareImage.size.width }}">
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="{{ shareImage.size.height }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% endif %}
    {% if isEntry %}
    {% if date %}
    <meta property="article:published_time" content="{{ date | date("iso") }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% if updated or date %}
    <meta property="article:modified_time" content="{{ (updated or date) | date("iso") }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% if siteAuthor.url %}
    <meta property="article:author" content="{{ siteAuthor.url | absoluteUrl }}">
    {% elif siteAuthor.name or author.name or author or site.author %}
    <meta property="article:author" content="{{ siteAuthor.name or author.name or author or site.author }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% for tag in tags %}
    <meta property="article:tag" content="{{ tag }}">
    {% endfor %}
    {% endif %}
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="{{ shareImage.card if shareImage else "summary" }}">
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ metaTitle }}">
    {% if metaDescription %}
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ metaDescription }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% if shareImage %}
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ shareImage.url | absoluteUrl }}">
    <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="{{ shareImage.alt }}">
    {% endif %}
    {#
      The site's icons, derived from its `icon` setting, else its avatar.
      `icons` is empty — and these lines print nothing — until a site has one,
      so a site with neither links no icon rather than links that answer 404.
      An SVG icon has every size, so it carries no `sizes`.
    #}
    {% for icon in icons %}
    <link rel="{{ icon.rel }}" type="{{ icon.type }}"{% if icon.sizes %} sizes="{{ icon.sizes }}"{% endif %} href="{{ icon.href | url }}">
    {% endfor %}
    <link rel="manifest" href="{{ "/manifest.webmanifest" | url }}">
    {% include "partials/jsonld.njk" %}
    {% endblock %}
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="screen-reader-text" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <div class="global-wrapper"{% if atRoot %} data-is-root-path="true"{% endif %}>
      {% block header %}
      {#
        The header, and the site menu inside it on every page (TASK-105): one
        place a reader looks for the navigation, whatever they are reading.

        The header is two shapes and the menu sits differently in each. At the
        root it is the site title and the tagline, and the menu is a line of
        its own under them; everywhere else it is the small link home, and the
        menu is beside it on that line. The stylesheet does the beside part,
        off `data-is-root-path` on the wrapper.
      #}
      <header class="global-header">
        {% if atRoot %}
        <h1 class="main-heading">
          <a href="{{ "/" | url }}">{{ site.title }}</a>
        </h1>
        {% if site.tagline %}
        <p class="site-tagline">{{ site.tagline }}</p>
        {% endif %}
        {% else %}
        <a class="header-link-home" href="{{ "/" | url }}">{{ site.title }}</a>
        {% endif %}
        {{ nav.list(menus.primary, "Site") }}
      </header>
      {% endblock %}

      <main id="main">
        {% block breadcrumbs %}{% include "partials/breadcrumbs.njk" %}{% endblock %}
        {% block content %}{% endblock %}
      </main>

      {% block footer %}
      <footer>
        <p>&copy; {{ "now" | date("year") }}{% if site.author %}, {{ site.author }}{% endif %} &middot; Published with Geekity</p>
        {#
          The footer menu: the `footer` area this theme declares in its
          `theme.json`, which a site fills in on the Navigation screen, and the
          whole of what the footer links (TASK-105). It is the site's own list
          — the feed, a colophon, a webring, a Mastodon link it wants verified
          — rather than anything read off an account: one nominated user's
          profile links presented as the site's were nobody's to give, and on a
          site with more than one person nobody else's appeared at all. An item
          marked `me` carries `rel="me"`, which is what a site moving a
          verified profile link in here needs.

          Nothing is printed until a site has filled the area in: an empty
          footer menu is no list rather than an empty one, and the copyright
          line above stands on its own.

          Webrings, badges and anything else that is markup rather than a link
          go in a site theme's `footer` override.
        #}
        {{ nav.list(menus.footer, "Footer") }}
      </footer>
      {% endblock %}
    </div>

    {% block scripts %}
    {#
      The code highlighter, and only where there is code to highlight.

      The CMS renders a fenced block as `pre > code.language-x` and highlights
      nothing, so that a theme picks its own highlighter and runs it on the
      client (decision-16). This one is highlight.js, self-hosted in
      `static/highlight.js` at a pinned version — nothing is loaded from a CDN.

      The test is the rendered body: a page whose `content` holds a
      `language-` class has a code block on it, and every other page — a
      listing, an entry with only prose — ships no JavaScript at all. `defer`
      so it waits for the markup it works on rather than blocking it.

      A site theme that wants a different highlighter, or none, overrides this
      block; one that wants a different set of languages rebuilds the bundle.
      See the theme README.
    #}
    {% if content and "language-" in content %}
    <script src="{{ "highlight.js" | asset }}" defer></script>
    {% endif %}
    {% endblock %}
  </body>
</html>
